  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe proposed approach combines multi-resolution optimization with a population-based strategy to explore the solution space effectively. We start with a coarse discretization to find a promising approximate solution and progressively refine it. This strategy helps escape local minima by allowing the search to focus on global structure first and then on fine details. The population-based component maintains multiple candidates, promoting diversification and robust exploration. The use of an adaptive learning rate with line search ensures efficient progress toward better solutions while maintaining feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nWe represent h as a vector of n_points samples, h \u2208 \u211d^n_points, with each h[i] \u2208 [0,1]. The integral constraint is \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2. The overlap for shift k is:\n\nC_k = sum_{i=0}^{n_points-1} h[i](1 - h[(i + k) % n_points]) * dx\n\nwhere dx = 2.0 / n_points. The objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max(C_k).\n\nWe maintain a population of P candidate solutions, each represented as a vector h^p, p = 1..P. Each candidate is kept feasible through a parametrization that ensures the constraints are satisfied without explicit projection.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Initialization:\n   i. If initial_h_values exist:\n      - Use it to initialize the population.\n      - For each candidate in the population, apply the parametrization to ensure feasibility.\n   ii. Else:\n      - Initialize with a triangular function.\n      - Generate additional candidates by perturbing the initial one.\n\nb. Population update:\n   i. For each candidate, compute the current C\u2085.\n   ii. Generate new candidates through mutation and crossover.\n   iii. Apply the parametrization to maintain feasibility.\n\nc. Gradient-based refinement:\n   i. For each candidate, compute the gradient of C\u2085 using central finite differences.\n   ii. Update the candidate with an adaptive step size, using line search to ensure improvement.\n\nd. Multi-resolution strategy:\n   i. Start with coarse grids (e.g., n_points=50) and gradually increase resolution.\n   ii. Transfer solutions from lower to higher resolutions using interpolation.\n\ne. Termination:\n   - Stop when the time budget is exhausted, returning the best feasible solution found.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- Population size P = 10 for balance between diversity and computation.\n- Coarse grid starts at n_points=50, refined in steps to n_points=200.\n- Mutation rate of 10%, with adaptive step sizes based on progress.\n- Line search with backtracking for gradient updates.\n- Parametrization using multinomial logistic to enforce constraints.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n-\u4eba\u53e3\u66f4\u65b0\u548c\u68af\u5ea6\u8ba1\u7b97\u786e\u4fdd\u4e86\u8ba1\u7b97\u6548\u7387\u3002\n-\u7ebf\u6027\u641c\u7d22\u548c\u81ea\u9002\u5e94\u5b66\u4e60\u7387\u9002\u5e94\u4e86\u4f18\u5316\u52a8\u529b\u5b66\u3002\n-\u8fdb\u9000\u7ebf\u641c\u7d22\u786e\u4fdd\u6bcf\u4e00\u6b65\u90fd\u6539\u8fdb\u76ee\u6807\u3002\n-\u79cd\u7fa4\u591a\u6837\u6027\u964d\u4f4e\u4e86\u9677\u5165\u5c40\u90e8\u6781\u5c0f\u503c\u7684\u98ce\u9669\u3002\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n-\u6bcf\u79cd\u5019\u9009\u65b9\u6848\u90fd\u7ecf\u8fc7\u9a8c\u8bc1\uff0c\u786e\u4fdd\u6ee1\u8db3\u7ea6\u675f\u3002\n-\u4f7f\u7528\u5b9e\u9645\u8bc4\u4f30\u5668\u91cd\u65b0\u8ba1\u7b97C\u2085\uff0c\u786e\u4fdd\u51c6\u786e\u6027\u3002\n-\u5728\u6700\u7ec8\u8fd4\u56de\u524d\uff0c\u9a8c\u8bc1\u6240\u6709h\u7684\u503c\u548c\u603b\u548c\u3002",
